// Package journal is a pure double-entry accounting primitive for realms
// that need books which provably balance. It is to *bookkeeping* what
// feeledger is to *money* and duebook is to *time*: it holds no coins,
// performs no effects, imports no chain APIs, and owns no package-level
// state. The importing realm owns a *Book, supplies the clock, and performs
// its own effects.
//
// # The one idea
//
// Every financial primitive in the searched Gno ecosystem is ONE-SIDED. A
// treasury records what moved. A pull-payment ledger records who is owed
// what. A GRC20 ledger records who holds what. Each stores a balance and
// mutates it.
//
// This package stores no balance. It stores ENTRIES, and derives balances
// from them:
//
//	book.MustOpen("1000", "Cash", Asset)
//	book.MustOpen("4000", "Revenue", Revenue)
//	book.MustPost(now, "invoice 17 paid", []Posting{
//		{Account: "1000", Side: Debit, Amount: 5000},
//		{Account: "4000", Side: Credit, Amount: 5000},
//	})
//
// An entry that does not balance is refused, and the refusal says which side
// is short. So `sum(debits) == sum(credits)` is not a property the consumer
// must remember to maintain — it is the precondition for the state changing
// at all. A Book cannot be put into an unbalanced state by any sequence of
// calls to this package.
//
// # No amend, no void
//
// Post is the only way in, and there is no Edit and no Delete. A mistake is
// corrected the way real books correct one: with a REVERSING ENTRY that
// mirrors every posting of the original, leaving both visible.
//
//	book.MustReverse(entryID, now, "wrong account")
//
// This is why history is trustworthy here and would not be if entries were
// mutable. An auditor reading the journal sees the error AND the correction,
// which is the entire point of keeping a journal rather than a balance.
// Reverse refuses a second reversal of the same entry, so a correction
// cannot be looped.
//
// # Periods
//
// Close(through) seals everything dated at or before `through`. It only ever
// moves forward, and after it Post refuses any entry dated into the sealed
// range — the backdating that makes a closed period meaningless. Reverse is
// refused there too: correcting a sealed period means opening a new entry in
// the current one, which is the consumer's call to make explicitly.
//
// # What "balanced" is actually worth
//
// Read this before quoting IsBalanced as a guarantee.
//
// IsBalanced compares TWO INDEPENDENTLY MAINTAINED ACCUMULATORS: the
// per-account debit/credit totals, summed across the chart, against the
// Book's own running totals. They are written by different code paths, so
// a drift between them is a real defect and IsBalanced finds it. What it
// does NOT do is re-read the journal, so a defect that corrupted both
// accumulators identically would survive it.
//
// RederiveFromJournal is the strong check: it replays every posting of every
// entry and compares the result against both accumulators. It costs O(all
// postings ever) and exists to be run deliberately, not on every read.
//
// AccountingEquation is WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS and is documented as such
// rather than left for a reader to assume otherwise: under this package's
// sign conventions it is implied by IsBalanced, and its boolean proves
// EXACTLY ONE of IsBalanced's three conjuncts. It is NOT an independent
// accounting fact. Three checks that are really two, presented as three,
// would be the defect this package exists to avoid.
//
// This doc said "it is a cross-check of this package's own normal-balance
// handling" until 2026-10-07, and that sentence was FALSE — the one place
// the careful disclaimer around it still overclaimed. The boolean is the
// sum of every account's RawDelta, bucketed by Kind and then added back
// together, so the bucketing cannot change it: offsets is INVARIANT under
// every possible Kind misclassification, which is the one thing
// "normal-balance handling" means. See obligation 7 below, and
// AccountingEquation's own doc for what it does prove.
//
// And the ceiling on all three: a Book can be internally perfect and
// describe nothing. See obligation 1 below.
//
// # State growth
//
// Entries are never removed, because an append-only journal whose entries
// could vanish is not a journal. Storage therefore grows with every Post and
// is bounded only by maxEntries, set at construction. A Book that reaches
// that cap refuses further entries rather than silently dropping history —
// a long-lived consumer must plan for this, and the plan is a new Book with
// opening balances carried forward, not a pruned one.
//
// # Consumer contract (the parts the package cannot enforce)
//
//  1. THE BOOKS ARE CLAIMS BY WHOEVER POSTED THEM. This package proves
//     arithmetic, never truth. With no custody there is no solvency
//     invariant tying "Cash 5000" to 5000 real ugnot anywhere. A realm that
//     presents a journal balance as a holding is asserting something this
//     package did not check. This is the single most important obligation
//     and the one most commonly got wrong.
//  2. SUPPLY A REAL CLOCK. journal cannot verify that `at` came from
//     runtime.ChainHeight() or time.Now().Unix(). A realm that lets a caller
//     choose `at` has no period discipline at all: the caller dates entries
//     wherever they are convenient. Pass the chain's clock, never a
//     transaction parameter.
//  3. USE ONE CLOCK CONSISTENTLY. Heights and seconds must not be mixed
//     within a Book; `at` and `through` are both in the caller's unit.
//  4. DO NOT EXPORT THE BOOK. A *Book is a mutable handle. Returning one
//     across a realm boundary hands out the right to open accounts, post and
//     reverse. Expose your own crossing functions instead; this package
//     returns Account and Entry VALUES, never pointers into its state.
//  5. AUTHORIZE THE POSTER. journal authenticates nobody. Who may open an
//     account, who may post, and who may close a period are the realm's
//     policy — derive the caller from cur.Previous().Address(), not from an
//     argument.
//  6. CHOOSE THE UNIT ONCE. Amounts are int64 in whatever minor unit the
//     consumer picks. journal does not know about denominations and will
//     happily balance an entry that mixes two of them.
//  7. GET THE KIND RIGHT AT OPEN TIME. NO CHECK IN THIS PACKAGE VALIDATES
//     AN ACCOUNT'S KIND. A Kind is accepted if it is one of the five and is
//     never revisited, so an account opened Asset that should have been
//     Liability reports a sign-flipped Balance() forever — and IsBalanced,
//     RederiveFromJournal and AccountingEquation all three stay healthy,
//     because every one of them is built on Debits and Credits, which the
//     Kind does not touch. This is the invariant tiers' blind spot, it is
//     structural rather than an omission to be fixed later, and a realm
//     that lets a caller choose the Kind has delegated a decision no
//     later check can audit.
//
// All failures are returned as errors and leave the Book COMPLETELY
// UNCHANGED. Must* wrappers are the only functions here that panic.
//
// The Book is address-agnostic: account codes are non-empty strings. Realms
// normally use a chart-of-accounts code, not an address.
package journal

import (
	"errors"
	"strconv"

	"gno.land/p/nt/avl/v0"
)

// MaxAccountsLimit is the largest maxAccounts a Book may be constructed
// with. It bounds the cost of IsBalanced and AccountingEquation, which scan
// the chart of accounts.
const MaxAccountsLimit = 10000

// MaxEntriesLimit is the largest maxEntries a Book may be constructed with.
const MaxEntriesLimit = 1000000

// MaxPostingsPerEntry bounds a single entry's posting count. Two is the
// minimum a balanced entry can have; the ceiling keeps one Post bounded.
const MaxPostingsPerEntry = 64

// MaxCodeLen bounds an account code.
const MaxCodeLen = 64

// MaxNameLen bounds an account's human-readable name.
const MaxNameLen = 256

// MaxMemoLen bounds an entry memo. The memo is opaque to journal — it exists
// so a realm can record why an entry was made without keeping a parallel
// table.
const MaxMemoLen = 1024

// Errors returned by Book operations.
var (
	ErrEmptyCode        = errors.New("journal: empty account code")
	ErrCodeTooLong      = errors.New("journal: account code exceeds MaxCodeLen")
	ErrNameTooLong      = errors.New("journal: account name exceeds MaxNameLen")
	ErrMemoTooLong      = errors.New("journal: memo exceeds MaxMemoLen")
	ErrInvalidKind      = errors.New("journal: unknown account kind")
	ErrInvalidSide      = errors.New("journal: posting side must be Debit or Credit")
	ErrInvalidAmount    = errors.New("journal: posting amount must be positive")
	ErrInvalidAt        = errors.New("journal: at must be non-negative")
	ErrInvalidConfig    = errors.New("journal: invalid book configuration")
	ErrAccountExists    = errors.New("journal: account code already open")
	ErrUnknownAccount   = errors.New("journal: no such account")
	ErrChartFull        = errors.New("journal: account cap reached")
	ErrBookFull         = errors.New("journal: entry cap reached")
	ErrTooFewPostings   = errors.New("journal: an entry needs at least two postings")
	ErrTooManyPostings  = errors.New("journal: entry exceeds MaxPostingsPerEntry")
	ErrNoDebit          = errors.New("journal: entry has no debit posting")
	ErrNoCredit         = errors.New("journal: entry has no credit posting")
	ErrDebitShort       = errors.New("journal: entry does not balance, debits are short")
	ErrCreditShort      = errors.New("journal: entry does not balance, credits are short")
	ErrEntryNotFound    = errors.New("journal: no such entry")
	ErrAlreadyReversed  = errors.New("journal: entry has already been reversed")
	ErrPeriodClosed     = errors.New("journal: at falls in a closed period")
	ErrCloseNotForward  = errors.New("journal: close must move forward")
	ErrOverflow         = errors.New("journal: int64 overflow")
	ErrIDExhausted      = errors.New("journal: identifier space exhausted")
)

// Kind is an account's classification, which fixes its normal balance.
// The zero value is deliberately invalid so a forgotten field is an error
// rather than an Asset.
type Kind uint8

// The five account kinds. Asset and Expense are debit-normal; Liability,
// Equity and Revenue are credit-normal.
const (
	Asset     Kind = 1
	Liability Kind = 2
	Equity    Kind = 3
	Revenue   Kind = 4
	Expense   Kind = 5
)

// Valid reports whether k is one of the five kinds.
func (k Kind) Valid() bool { return k >= Asset && k <= Expense }

// IsDebitNormal reports whether an increase in k is recorded as a debit.
// Asset and Expense are debit-normal; the other three are credit-normal.
// An invalid Kind reports false, which is why callers validate first.
func (k Kind) IsDebitNormal() bool { return k == Asset || k == Expense }

// String returns the kind's name, or "invalid" for the zero value and any
// unrecognised code.
func (k Kind) String() string {
	switch k {
	case Asset:
		return "asset"
	case Liability:
		return "liability"
	case Equity:
		return "equity"
	case Revenue:
		return "revenue"
	case Expense:
		return "expense"
	}
	return "invalid"
}

// Side is which column a posting lands in. The zero value is deliberately
// invalid: a posting with a forgotten Side must be refused, not silently
// treated as a debit.
type Side uint8

// The two posting sides.
const (
	Debit  Side = 1
	Credit Side = 2
)

// Valid reports whether s is Debit or Credit.
func (s Side) Valid() bool { return s == Debit || s == Credit }

// String returns "debit", "credit", or "invalid".
func (s Side) String() string {
	switch s {
	case Debit:
		return "debit"
	case Credit:
		return "credit"
	}
	return "invalid"
}

// Opposite returns the other side. An invalid Side returns itself, so a
// caller that skipped validation gets an invalid posting rather than a
// plausible one.
func (s Side) Opposite() Side {
	switch s {
	case Debit:
		return Credit
	case Credit:
		return Debit
	}
	return s
}

// Posting is one leg of an entry: an amount in one column of one account.
// Amount is always POSITIVE — direction is carried by Side, not by a sign.
//
// That is a deliberate choice over signed amounts. With a sign, "which side
// is short" is unanswerable without knowing each account's normal balance,
// so an unbalanced entry can only be reported as a number that does not add
// up. With an explicit Side the refusal names the short column, which is
// what a bookkeeper needs in order to fix it.
type Posting struct {
	Account string
	Side    Side
	Amount  int64
}

// Account is one line of the chart of accounts, carrying its cumulative
// debit and credit totals. It is returned BY VALUE: holders cannot reach
// into a Book through it.
type Account struct {
	Code    string
	Name    string
	Kind    Kind
	Debits  int64
	Credits int64
}

// Balance returns the account's balance in its NORMAL direction: for a
// debit-normal account Debits-Credits, for a credit-normal account
// Credits-Debits. A normal account therefore has a non-negative balance,
// and a negative result is a real signal (a contra position) rather than a
// sign convention.
func (a Account) Balance() int64 {
	if a.Kind.IsDebitNormal() {
		return a.Debits - a.Credits
	}
	return a.Credits - a.Debits
}

// RawDelta returns Debits-Credits regardless of kind. Summed over every
// account in a balanced book it is exactly zero, which is what makes it the
// useful quantity for a consistency check and the wrong one for a report.
func (a Account) RawDelta() int64 { return a.Debits - a.Credits }

// Entry is one journal entry: a dated, memo'd, balanced set of postings.
// It is returned BY VALUE, and its Postings slice is a DEFENSIVE COPY — a
// holder can mutate the returned slice without touching the Book. Entry is
// the one type here that contains a slice, so it is the one type where
// returning the stored value directly would have handed out a writable
// window into Book state.
//
// ReversalOf is 0 unless this entry was produced by Reverse, in which case
// it is the ID of the entry it mirrors.
type Entry struct {
	ID         uint64
	At         int64
	Memo       string
	Postings   []Posting
	ReversalOf uint64
}

// IsReversal reports whether this entry was produced by Reverse.
func (e Entry) IsReversal() bool { return e.ReversalOf != 0 }

// Total returns the entry's debit total, which for any entry a Book accepted
// equals its credit total.
func (e Entry) Total() int64 {
	var t int64
	for _, p := range e.Postings {
		if p.Side == Debit {
			t += p.Amount
		}
	}
	return t
}

// Book is the chart of accounts and the journal of one consuming realm. The
// zero value is not usable; construct with New.
type Book struct {
	maxAccounts int
	maxEntries  int

	accounts *avl.Tree // code -> Account
	entries  *avl.Tree // padded id -> Entry
	// reversed stores `true`, not a struct{} sentinel, and deliberately:
	// avl.Tree.Get cannot tell a nil-valued key from an absent one, so a
	// nil sentinel invites a future reader to switch from Has to Get and
	// get a silently wrong answer. Membership is the whole value here —
	// both readers use Has and the stored value is never read.
	reversed *avl.Tree // padded id -> true, for entries already reversed

	nextID        uint64 // never decreases; entry IDs are never reused
	closedThrough int64  // entries dated at or before this are refused
	hasClosed     bool   // distinguishes "closed through 0" from "never closed"

	totalDebits  int64 // running total, maintained independently of accounts
	totalCredits int64
}

// New returns an empty Book.
//
// maxAccounts caps the chart and must be in [1, MaxAccountsLimit].
// maxEntries caps the journal and must be in [1, MaxEntriesLimit]; because
// entries are never removed, it is a lifetime cap rather than a concurrent
// one, and a Book that reaches it is finished.
func New(maxAccounts, maxEntries int) (*Book, error) {
	if maxAccounts < 1 || maxAccounts > MaxAccountsLimit {
		return nil, ErrInvalidConfig
	}
	if maxEntries < 1 || maxEntries > MaxEntriesLimit {
		return nil, ErrInvalidConfig
	}
	return &Book{
		maxAccounts: maxAccounts,
		maxEntries:  maxEntries,
		accounts:    avl.NewTree(),
		entries:     avl.NewTree(),
		reversed:    avl.NewTree(),
		nextID:      1,
	}, nil
}

// MustNew is New but panics on error.
func MustNew(maxAccounts, maxEntries int) *Book {
	b, err := New(maxAccounts, maxEntries)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return b
}

// MaxAccounts returns the Book's cap on chart size.
func (b *Book) MaxAccounts() int { return b.maxAccounts }

// MaxEntries returns the Book's lifetime cap on entries.
func (b *Book) MaxEntries() int { return b.maxEntries }

// AccountCount returns how many accounts are open.
func (b *Book) AccountCount() int { return b.accounts.Size() }

// EntryCount returns how many entries the journal holds. It never decreases.
func (b *Book) EntryCount() int { return b.entries.Size() }

// NextID returns the identifier the next entry will take. It only ever
// increases, so an entry ID identifies one entry for the life of the Book.
func (b *Book) NextID() uint64 { return b.nextID }

// TotalDebits returns the Book's running debit total across every entry.
func (b *Book) TotalDebits() int64 { return b.totalDebits }

// TotalCredits returns the Book's running credit total across every entry.
func (b *Book) TotalCredits() int64 { return b.totalCredits }

// ClosedThrough returns the period seal: entries dated at or before it are
// refused. The second result is false if the Book has never been closed, in
// which case the first is meaningless — a Book closed through 0 and a Book
// never closed differ, and collapsing them would silently reject entries
// dated at 0.
func (b *Book) ClosedThrough() (int64, bool) { return b.closedThrough, b.hasClosed }

// Open adds an account to the chart. Codes are unique and immutable: there
// is no Rename and no Close, because an account's cumulative totals are part
// of the derivation of every balance that cites it, and removing one would
// make the journal unreplayable.
//
// Fails with ErrEmptyCode, ErrCodeTooLong, ErrNameTooLong, ErrInvalidKind,
// ErrAccountExists, or ErrChartFull. On error nothing is modified.
func (b *Book) Open(code, name string, kind Kind) error {
	if code == "" {
		return ErrEmptyCode
	}
	if len(code) > MaxCodeLen {
		return ErrCodeTooLong
	}
	if len(name) > MaxNameLen {
		return ErrNameTooLong
	}
	if !kind.Valid() {
		return ErrInvalidKind
	}
	if b.accounts.Has(code) {
		return ErrAccountExists
	}
	if b.accounts.Size() >= b.maxAccounts {
		return ErrChartFull
	}
	b.accounts.Set(code, Account{Code: code, Name: name, Kind: kind})
	return nil
}

// MustOpen is Open but panics on error.
func (b *Book) MustOpen(code, name string, kind Kind) {
	if err := b.Open(code, name, kind); err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
}

// Account returns an account by code. The second result is false if no such
// account is open.
func (b *Book) Account(code string) (Account, bool) { return b.account(code) }

// Balance returns an account's balance in its normal direction, and whether
// the account exists. A missing account returns (0, false) rather than a
// zero balance, because "no such account" and "balance zero" are different
// answers and a consumer branching on the number alone would conflate them.
func (b *Book) Balance(code string) (int64, bool) {
	a, ok := b.account(code)
	if !ok {
		return 0, false
	}
	return a.Balance(), true
}

// Post appends a balanced entry and returns its ID.
//
// The postings must number at least two and at most MaxPostingsPerEntry,
// each naming an open account, a valid Side, and a positive Amount. The
// entry must contain at least one debit and at least one credit, and the two
// column totals must be equal — an inequality is reported as ErrDebitShort
// or ErrCreditShort, naming the column that needs more.
//
// Fails with ErrInvalidAt, ErrMemoTooLong, ErrPeriodClosed, ErrBookFull,
// ErrIDExhausted, ErrTooFewPostings, ErrTooManyPostings, ErrInvalidSide,
// ErrInvalidAmount, ErrUnknownAccount, ErrNoDebit, ErrNoCredit,
// ErrDebitShort, ErrCreditShort, or ErrOverflow. On error nothing is
// modified — every posting is validated and every total computed before the
// first write.
func (b *Book) Post(at int64, memo string, postings []Posting) (uint64, error) {
	if at < 0 {
		return 0, ErrInvalidAt
	}
	if len(memo) > MaxMemoLen {
		return 0, ErrMemoTooLong
	}
	if b.hasClosed && at <= b.closedThrough {
		return 0, ErrPeriodClosed
	}
	return b.post(at, memo, 0, postings)
}

// MustPost is Post but panics on error.
func (b *Book) MustPost(at int64, memo string, postings []Posting) uint64 {
	id, err := b.Post(at, memo, postings)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return id
}

// Reverse appends the mirror of an existing entry — every posting repeated
// on the opposite side, for the same amount — and returns the new entry's
// ID. The original is left exactly as it was.
//
// An entry may be reversed at most once. Reversing a reversal IS permitted
// (it re-applies the original's effect) and is itself subject to the same
// once-only rule, so a correction cannot be looped.
//
// `at` dates the correction and must be in the open period: a sealed period
// cannot be corrected in place, by construction. Correcting one means
// posting into the current period, which is the consumer's decision to make
// explicitly rather than this package's to make silently.
//
// Fails with ErrInvalidAt, ErrMemoTooLong, ErrPeriodClosed,
// ErrEntryNotFound, ErrAlreadyReversed, ErrBookFull, ErrIDExhausted, or
// ErrOverflow. On error nothing is modified.
func (b *Book) Reverse(id uint64, at int64, memo string) (uint64, error) {
	if at < 0 {
		return 0, ErrInvalidAt
	}
	if len(memo) > MaxMemoLen {
		return 0, ErrMemoTooLong
	}
	if b.hasClosed && at <= b.closedThrough {
		return 0, ErrPeriodClosed
	}
	orig, ok := b.entry(id)
	if !ok {
		return 0, ErrEntryNotFound
	}
	if b.reversed.Has(idKey(id)) {
		return 0, ErrAlreadyReversed
	}

	mirror := make([]Posting, len(orig.Postings))
	for i, p := range orig.Postings {
		mirror[i] = Posting{Account: p.Account, Side: p.Side.Opposite(), Amount: p.Amount}
	}

	newID, err := b.post(at, memo, id, mirror)
	if err != nil {
		return 0, err
	}
	// Marked only after post succeeded, so a refused reversal does not
	// consume the original's single reversal.
	b.reversed.Set(idKey(id), true)
	return newID, nil
}

// MustReverse is Reverse but panics on error.
func (b *Book) MustReverse(id uint64, at int64, memo string) uint64 {
	newID, err := b.Reverse(id, at, memo)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return newID
}

// IsReversed reports whether an entry has already been reversed. It returns
// false for an entry that does not exist, so pair it with Entry when the
// distinction matters.
func (b *Book) IsReversed(id uint64) bool { return b.reversed.Has(idKey(id)) }

// Close seals the period ending at `through`, after which Post and Reverse
// refuse any entry dated at or before it.
//
// It only moves forward. A first close may name any non-negative instant; a
// later one must name a strictly greater instant, so a seal can never be
// loosened and backdating can never be re-enabled.
//
// Fails with ErrInvalidAt (through < 0) or ErrCloseNotForward. On error
// nothing is modified.
func (b *Book) Close(through int64) error {
	if through < 0 {
		return ErrInvalidAt
	}
	if b.hasClosed && through <= b.closedThrough {
		return ErrCloseNotForward
	}
	b.closedThrough = through
	b.hasClosed = true
	return nil
}

// MustClose is Close but panics on error.
func (b *Book) MustClose(through int64) {
	if err := b.Close(through); err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
}

// Entry returns an entry by ID, with a defensive copy of its postings. The
// second result is false if no such entry exists — which, because entries
// are never removed, means it was never created.
func (b *Book) Entry(id uint64) (Entry, bool) {
	e, ok := b.entry(id)
	if !ok {
		return Entry{}, false
	}
	return e.withCopiedPostings(), true
}

// IsBalanced reports whether the Book's two independently-maintained
// accumulators agree with each other and with themselves.
//
// It scans the chart, sums every account's debit and credit totals, and
// requires that the two sums are equal AND that each matches the Book's own
// running total. Those totals are written by different code in Post, so a
// disagreement is a defect in this package and IsBalanced is how a consumer
// detects one.
//
// What it does not do is re-read the journal. Read the package doc's
// "What balanced is actually worth" before quoting it, and use
// RederiveFromJournal for the strong claim.
//
// An empty Book is balanced.
func (b *Book) IsBalanced() bool {
	d, c, ok := b.sumAccounts()
	if !ok {
		return false
	}
	return d == c && d == b.totalDebits && c == b.totalCredits
}

// RederiveFromJournal replays every posting of every entry and reports the
// debit and credit totals it finds, together with whether they agree with
// both the per-account accumulators and the Book's running totals.
//
// This is the strong check and the expensive one: its cost is proportional
// to every posting ever made, so it is meant to be called deliberately — by
// an auditor, or by a consumer's own invariant view — and not from a hot
// path. `agrees` false on a Book this package built is a defect in this
// package.
func (b *Book) RederiveFromJournal() (debits, credits int64, agrees bool) {
	var d, c int64
	okMath := true
	b.entries.Iterate("", "", func(_ string, value any) bool {
		e := value.(Entry)
		for _, p := range e.Postings {
			var ok bool
			if p.Side == Debit {
				d, ok = checkedAdd(d, p.Amount)
			} else {
				c, ok = checkedAdd(c, p.Amount)
			}
			if !ok {
				okMath = false
				return true
			}
		}
		return false
	})
	if !okMath {
		return 0, 0, false
	}
	sd, sc, okSum := b.sumAccounts()
	agrees = okSum &&
		d == c &&
		d == b.totalDebits && c == b.totalCredits &&
		d == sd && c == sc
	return d, c, agrees
}

// AccountingEquation returns the two halves of assets+expenses versus
// liabilities+equity+revenue, in RAW debit-minus-credit terms, and whether
// they offset.
//
// IT IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS, and weaker than this doc used to claim.
//
// What offsets ACTUALLY proves: sum(RawDelta) == 0 over the whole chart,
// which is the same fact as IsBalanced's first conjunct (d == c) and
// nothing more. IsBalanced requires three. So this is implied by
// IsBalanced, is NOT an independent accounting fact, and citing it as a
// second guarantee alongside IsBalanced is counting one check twice.
//
// What offsets DOES NOT prove, despite the Kind partition sitting right
// there in the code: anything whatsoever about normal-balance handling.
// dn and cn are a partition of the same addends, so dn+cn is the total
// regardless of which bucket each account landed in. Reclassify every
// account in the chart and BOTH subtotals change while offsets stays
// true. A Kind set wrongly at Open time is undetectable here and
// undetectable anywhere else in this package — obligation 7.
//
// It is exported because the two SUBTOTALS are correct and a consumer
// presenting a balance sheet wants them anyway. They are the product;
// offsets is a duplicate of a check you already have.
func (b *Book) AccountingEquation() (debitNormal, creditNormal int64, offsets bool) {
	var dn, cn int64
	okMath := true
	b.accounts.Iterate("", "", func(_ string, value any) bool {
		a := value.(Account)
		var ok bool
		if a.Kind.IsDebitNormal() {
			dn, ok = checkedAdd(dn, a.RawDelta())
		} else {
			cn, ok = checkedAdd(cn, a.RawDelta())
		}
		if !ok {
			okMath = false
			return true
		}
		return false
	})
	if !okMath {
		return 0, 0, false
	}
	sum, ok := checkedAdd(dn, cn)
	return dn, cn, ok && sum == 0
}

// IterateAccounts calls fn for every account in ascending code order.
// Iteration stops early when fn returns true.
//
// fn receives a COPY: Account is passed by value and holds only scalars and
// strings, so fn gets no pointer into the Book.
//
// Two rules for fn, and the second is the one that is easy to miss.
//
//   - Do not Open, Post, Reverse or Close from inside fn: mutating a tree
//     while iterating it is undefined. Collect what you need first, then act
//     after IterateAccounts returns.
//
//   - fn runs under the CALLING REALM'S STORAGE AUTHORITY. This method's
//     receiver is stamped with the importing realm's PkgID, so the borrow
//     rules leave the realm context set to that realm for the whole
//     callback, and a top-level fn has no receiver and no declaring realm to
//     anchor it elsewhere. A callback that re-enters the calling realm's own
//     mutators therefore does so with that realm's authority. Never pass a
//     caller-supplied function here from inside a permission-gated path;
//     pass only a closure this package's consumer wrote itself.
func (b *Book) IterateAccounts(fn func(Account) bool) {
	b.accounts.Iterate("", "", func(_ string, value any) bool {
		return fn(value.(Account))
	})
}

// IterateEntries calls fn for every entry in ascending ID order, which for
// an append-only journal is also chronological by insertion. Iteration stops
// early when fn returns true.
//
// fn receives an Entry whose Postings slice is a DEFENSIVE COPY, so a
// callback cannot write through it into Book state. The two rules on
// IterateAccounts's fn apply here unchanged, including the storage-authority
// one.
func (b *Book) IterateEntries(fn func(Entry) bool) {
	b.entries.Iterate("", "", func(_ string, value any) bool {
		return fn(value.(Entry).withCopiedPostings())
	})
}

// Entries returns up to limit entries with ID >= from, in ascending ID
// order, each with a defensive copy of its postings. A limit <= 0 returns
// nothing. It is the paging read for a consumer that cannot hold the whole
// journal in one call.
func (b *Book) Entries(from uint64, limit int) []Entry {
	out := []Entry{}
	if limit <= 0 {
		return out
	}
	b.entries.Iterate(idKey(from), "", func(_ string, value any) bool {
		out = append(out, value.(Entry).withCopiedPostings())
		return len(out) >= limit
	})
	return out
}

// post is the shared body of Post and Reverse. Callers have already
// validated `at`, the memo, and the period seal; reversalOf is 0 for an
// original entry. It validates every posting and computes both totals before
// writing anything.
func (b *Book) post(at int64, memo string, reversalOf uint64, postings []Posting) (uint64, error) {
	if len(postings) < 2 {
		return 0, ErrTooFewPostings
	}
	if len(postings) > MaxPostingsPerEntry {
		return 0, ErrTooManyPostings
	}
	if b.entries.Size() >= b.maxEntries {
		return 0, ErrBookFull
	}
	if b.nextID == 0 {
		return 0, ErrIDExhausted
	}

	var debits, credits int64
	sawDebit, sawCredit := false, false
	for _, p := range postings {
		if !p.Side.Valid() {
			return 0, ErrInvalidSide
		}
		if p.Amount <= 0 {
			return 0, ErrInvalidAmount
		}
		if !b.accounts.Has(p.Account) {
			return 0, ErrUnknownAccount
		}
		var ok bool
		if p.Side == Debit {
			sawDebit = true
			debits, ok = checkedAdd(debits, p.Amount)
		} else {
			sawCredit = true
			credits, ok = checkedAdd(credits, p.Amount)
		}
		if !ok {
			return 0, ErrOverflow
		}
	}
	if !sawDebit {
		return 0, ErrNoDebit
	}
	if !sawCredit {
		return 0, ErrNoCredit
	}
	if debits < credits {
		return 0, ErrDebitShort
	}
	if credits < debits {
		return 0, ErrCreditShort
	}

	// Book-level running totals, checked before any account is touched.
	newTotalDebits, ok := checkedAdd(b.totalDebits, debits)
	if !ok {
		return 0, ErrOverflow
	}
	newTotalCredits, ok := checkedAdd(b.totalCredits, credits)
	if !ok {
		return 0, ErrOverflow
	}

	// Per-account accumulators, all computed into a staging list before the
	// first Set, so an overflow on the last posting leaves nothing written.
	staged := make([]Account, len(postings))
	for i, p := range postings {
		a, found := b.account(p.Account)
		if !found {
			// Unreachable: Has() succeeded above and nothing has been
			// written since. Refused rather than ignored, because a silent
			// zero Account here would corrupt the chart.
			return 0, ErrUnknownAccount
		}
		// Fold earlier stagings of the same account, so an entry that posts
		// to one account twice accumulates both legs instead of the second
		// staging overwriting the first.
		for j := 0; j < i; j++ {
			if staged[j].Code == p.Account {
				a = staged[j]
			}
		}
		var okAcc bool
		if p.Side == Debit {
			a.Debits, okAcc = checkedAdd(a.Debits, p.Amount)
		} else {
			a.Credits, okAcc = checkedAdd(a.Credits, p.Amount)
		}
		if !okAcc {
			return 0, ErrOverflow
		}
		staged[i] = a
	}

	// Past here nothing can fail.
	id := b.nextID
	b.nextID++
	stored := make([]Posting, len(postings))
	copy(stored, postings)
	b.entries.Set(idKey(id), Entry{
		ID:         id,
		At:         at,
		Memo:       memo,
		Postings:   stored,
		ReversalOf: reversalOf,
	})
	for _, a := range staged {
		b.accounts.Set(a.Code, a)
	}
	b.totalDebits = newTotalDebits
	b.totalCredits = newTotalCredits
	return id, nil
}

// sumAccounts totals every account's debit and credit columns. The bool is
// false on overflow, which is why callers must not treat (0, 0) as an empty
// chart.
func (b *Book) sumAccounts() (debits, credits int64, ok bool) {
	var d, c int64
	okMath := true
	b.accounts.Iterate("", "", func(_ string, value any) bool {
		a := value.(Account)
		var okAdd bool
		if d, okAdd = checkedAdd(d, a.Debits); !okAdd {
			okMath = false
			return true
		}
		if c, okAdd = checkedAdd(c, a.Credits); !okAdd {
			okMath = false
			return true
		}
		return false
	})
	if !okMath {
		return 0, 0, false
	}
	return d, c, true
}

// account looks up one chart line.
func (b *Book) account(code string) (Account, bool) {
	v := b.accounts.Get(code)
	if v == nil {
		return Account{}, false
	}
	return v.(Account), true
}

// entry looks up one journal entry, WITHOUT copying its postings. Internal
// callers must not retain or mutate the returned slice; every exported path
// goes through withCopiedPostings.
func (b *Book) entry(id uint64) (Entry, bool) {
	v := b.entries.Get(idKey(id))
	if v == nil {
		return Entry{}, false
	}
	return v.(Entry), true
}

// withCopiedPostings returns e with a fresh Postings slice, so a value
// handed to a caller shares no backing array with the Book.
func (e Entry) withCopiedPostings() Entry {
	out := make([]Posting, len(e.Postings))
	copy(out, e.Postings)
	e.Postings = out
	return e
}

// idKey encodes an ID so that avl's lexical key order matches numeric ID
// order. math.MaxUint64 is 20 digits, so a fixed 20-wide zero-padded
// decimal is both sufficient and unambiguous.
func idKey(id uint64) string {
	s := strconv.FormatUint(id, 10)
	const width = 20
	if len(s) >= width {
		return s
	}
	return zeros[:width-len(s)] + s
}

const zeros = "00000000000000000000"

// checkedAdd returns a+b and reports whether the addition did not overflow
// int64.
func checkedAdd(a, b int64) (int64, bool) {
	sum := a + b
	if (b > 0 && sum < a) || (b < 0 && sum > a) {
		return 0, false
	}
	return sum, true
}

// Package books is a per-owner general ledger: an address opens a chart of
// accounts and posts balanced double-entry entries against it.
//
// # The one idea
//
// Every other ledger-like realm in this portfolio is SINGLE-PURPOSE and its
// state shape IS its question. invoices tracks receivables. payroll tracks
// accrued wages. budgets tracks allocations. fee_split tracks shares. Each is
// a correct answer to one question and structurally cannot answer a second,
// because there is nowhere to put a transaction that is not already one of
// the modelled kinds.
//
// This realm has no opinion about what the accounts mean. The owner defines
// the chart; the realm enforces that every entry balances, that it cannot be
// altered after the fact, and that only the owner may write it.
//
// # NO CUSTODY — and refusing coins is not the same as not holding them
//
// This realm holds nothing. There is no coinio import, no payable path, no
// fee, no withdrawal, no admin.
//
// So every mutating entrypoint REFUSES a transaction that arrives carrying
// coins. A realm with no withdrawal path that silently accepts ugnot strands
// it permanently, and "we don't hold funds" would then be true of the design
// and false of the balance. Having no custody is not the same as being safe
// to send coins to, and the difference is one guard.
//
// # A BOOK IS A SET OF CLAIMS BY ITS OWNER
//
// Read this before trusting any number this realm returns.
//
// With no custody there is no solvency invariant tying "Cash 5000" to 5000
// real ugnot anywhere. A book can be internally perfect and describe nothing.
// IsBalanced proves arithmetic, never truth.
//
// Consequently this realm exposes NO CROSS-OWNER AGGREGATE. Summing every
// book's debits would be summing every stranger's self-reported assertion,
// and the getter's existence would invite reading it as a signal. What it
// exposes instead is owner-scoped, which is the only question a consumer can
// act on — and a consumer acting on its OWN book is trusting only itself.
//
// # The account code DETERMINES the account kind
//
// An account's code's leading digit fixes its kind, by the standard
// chart-of-accounts convention:
//
//	1xxx -> Asset       2xxx -> Liability   3xxx -> Equity
//	4xxx -> Revenue     5xxx -> Expense
//
// There is no kind parameter. This is deliberate and it is the realm's answer
// to p/journal consumer obligation 7, which exists because of that package's
// audit finding Y1: NO CHECK IN p/journal VALIDATES AN ACCOUNT'S KIND. An
// account opened Asset that should have been Liability reports a sign-flipped
// Balance() forever while IsBalanced, RederiveFromJournal and
// AccountingEquation all three stay healthy — every one of them is built on
// Debits and Credits, which the kind does not touch.
//
// Deriving the kind does not eliminate that hazard. It MOVES it: after the
// derivation the kind cannot disagree with the code, so the unauditable
// question "is this kind right?" becomes the visible question "is this code
// right?", which any reader of the chart can check against the convention
// above. That is the whole claim and it is deliberately not stated as more.
//
// The cost is a mandatory numbering convention: a code whose first character
// is not 1-5 is refused. For a realm whose purpose is auditable books, that
// is the feature.
//
// # Entries are append-only and are corrected by REVERSAL
//
// There is no edit and no delete. A wrong entry is corrected by posting its
// mirror image, which leaves both the error and the correction readable. Each
// entry may be reversed at most once; a reversal is itself an entry and is
// itself reversible once.
//
// # Periods
//
// An owner may seal everything up to a height with CloseThrough, after which
// no entry may be dated at or before it. The seal only moves forward. This is
// the owner's own discipline, not a protocol rule — nothing forces an owner to
// close anything.
//
// # The clock is the chain's
//
// Entries are dated with runtime.ChainHeight() and never with a caller-
// supplied value, satisfying p/journal obligations 2 and 3. Heights
// throughout, never seconds. A realm that let a caller choose the date would
// have no period discipline at all.
//
// # Caps, and why they are far below the engine's
//
// p/journal permits 10 000 accounts and 1 000 000 entries. Its AUDIT.md names
// the gas ceiling on RederiveFromJournal at full cap — a worst case of 64M
// posting visits in one call — as UNMEASURED and does not claim it. So this
// realm sets its own caps far below, and exposes Rederive as an explicit
// owner-callable rather than running it inside a getter where another party
// would pay for it.
//
// The caps are per-owner, never global. A global cap on books would be a
// griefing vector: one address could fill it and lock everyone else out.
// Per-owner, an owner can exhaust their own capacity and nobody else's, and
// the cost of doing so is gas plus a storage deposit per record.
//
// # Realms may keep books too, for free
//
// The owner is cur.Previous().Address(), so a realm that calls in keeps books
// under its own address with no extra authorization code. That is the
// realm-to-realm integration surface, and it arrived as a property of the
// owner-only rule rather than as a feature.
//
// # What this realm does NOT do
//
//   - It does not hold, move, or price any asset.
//   - It does not verify that an entry describes a real event.
//   - It does not validate that the right transaction went in the right
//     account. The derived kind makes the CODE auditable, not the judgement.
//   - It has no admin. The deploying namespace has no privilege here at all:
//     no setter, no pause, no ability to post, reverse, or read anything a
//     stranger cannot read.
package books

import (
	"chain/runtime"
	"chain/runtime/unsafe"
	"strconv"
	"strings"

	"gno.land/p/g1ut6uspuh73e02yauxpmyt8g3wwddaq8utagvm3/journal"
	"gno.land/p/nt/avl/v0"
	"gno.land/p/nt/markdown/sanitize/v0"
)

// RealmPath is this realm's own path, used to build Render links.
const RealmPath = "/r/g1ut6uspuh73e02yauxpmyt8g3wwddaq8utagvm3/books"

// MaxAccountsPerBook caps one owner's chart. Far below journal's
// MaxAccountsLimit of 10000: IsBalanced sums the whole chart, and this bound
// is what keeps that read cheap for everyone.
const MaxAccountsPerBook = 128

// MaxEntriesPerBook caps one owner's journal. Far below journal's
// MaxEntriesLimit of 1000000, because that limit's interaction with
// RederiveFromJournal is unmeasured — see the package doc.
//
// A book that fills up refuses further entries rather than dropping history.
// The remedy is a fresh book with opening balances carried forward; there is
// deliberately no Archive, because it would destroy history and needs more
// design than it has had.
const MaxEntriesPerBook = 4096

// MaxLegsPerEntry caps one entry's postings. journal permits 64; this is
// lower because an entry is parsed from a single string argument and rendered
// in full, and both of those costs are linear in the leg count.
const MaxLegsPerEntry = 16

// MaxCodeLen and MaxNameLen bound an account's identifiers. Both are at or
// below journal's own limits, so the engine's validation is a backstop here
// rather than the only check.
const (
	MaxCodeLen = 16
	MaxNameLen = 128
)

// MaxMemoLen bounds an entry's free text. Below journal's MaxMemoLen of 1024
// because every memo is a candidate for Render output.
const MaxMemoLen = 256

// RenderEntryLimit caps how many entries one Render call emits. A book may
// hold MaxEntriesPerBook of them and rendering all of them would make the
// page cost grow without bound.
const RenderEntryLimit = 25

// books maps an owner's address string to that owner's *journal.Book.
//
// The value is a POINTER and is never returned by any exported function —
// journal consumer obligation 4: a *Book is a mutable handle, and handing one
// across a realm boundary would hand out the right to post and reverse.
var books = avl.NewTree()

// openedAt records the height each book was created, for Render.
var openedAt = avl.NewTree()

// bookCount is maintained alongside the tree so the index page never has to
// iterate it.
var bookCount int

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Guards
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// refuseCoins panics if a user call arrives carrying coins.
//
// This realm has no withdrawal path, so accepted coins would be stranded
// permanently. The guard is on IsUserCall rather than IsUser deliberately:
// OriginSend is only meaningful for a direct MsgCall, and the MsgRun
// ephemeral can consume the envelope before forwarding control, so IsUser
// would be the wrong predicate. See p/journal's audit note and security.md.
func refuseCoins(cur realm) {
	// A no-op at every one of today's call sites: each is a crossing entrypoint
	// forwarding its OWN first cur, which is runtime-current by construction
	// and stays current through a non-crossing call. It is here because this
	// realm is IMMUTABLE after addpkg, so a call site added later cannot be
	// made safe later — and a helper that reads Previous() off an unchecked
	// realm parameter is a designation-forgery shape (security.md Class 2)
	// whether or not it is reachable today.
	//
	// No count of the call sites is written here, per CLAUDE.md § 4a: the
	// number is the one thing about this comment that an edit elsewhere in the
	// file can falsify.
	if !cur.IsCurrent() {
		panic("books: refuseCoins called with a realm that is not current")
	}
	if cur.Previous().IsUserCall() && len(unsafe.OriginSend()) > 0 {
		panic("books: this realm holds no coins and has no withdrawal path; send none")
	}
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Account kinds, derived from the code
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// kindFromCode maps a code's leading digit to its account kind. The second
// result is false for any code that does not begin 1-5.
//
// This is the whole of the realm's answer to journal obligation 7. See the
// package doc for what it does and does not buy.
func kindFromCode(code string) (journal.Kind, bool) {
	if code == "" {
		return 0, false
	}
	switch code[0] {
	case '1':
		return journal.Asset, true
	case '2':
		return journal.Liability, true
	case '3':
		return journal.Equity, true
	case '4':
		return journal.Revenue, true
	case '5':
		return journal.Expense, true
	}
	return 0, false
}

// KindOf reports the kind a code would be given, without opening anything.
// A consumer can call this before OpenAccount to check its own numbering.
func KindOf(code string) string {
	k, ok := kindFromCode(code)
	if !ok {
		return "invalid"
	}
	return k.String()
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Writes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// OpenBook creates the caller's book. It panics if the caller already has one.
//
// Separating this from OpenAccount is deliberate: creating a book is the call
// that pays the storage deposit for the owner's record, and a caller should
// see that cost on its own rather than hidden inside their first account.
func OpenBook(cur realm) {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	owner := cur.Previous().Address()
	key := owner.String()
	if books.Has(key) {
		panic("books: this address already has a book")
	}
	b := journal.MustNew(MaxAccountsPerBook, MaxEntriesPerBook)
	books.Set(key, b)
	openedAt.Set(key, runtime.ChainHeight())
	bookCount++
}

// OpenAccount adds an account to the caller's book. The KIND IS DERIVED from
// the code's leading digit; there is no kind parameter. See the package doc.
func OpenAccount(cur realm, code, name string) {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	b := mustOwnBook(cur)

	if code == "" {
		panic("books: account code must not be empty")
	}
	if len(code) > MaxCodeLen {
		panic("books: account code too long, max " + strconv.Itoa(MaxCodeLen))
	}
	if len(name) > MaxNameLen {
		panic("books: account name too long, max " + strconv.Itoa(MaxNameLen))
	}
	kind, ok := kindFromCode(code)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: account code must begin 1-5 (1 asset, 2 liability, " +
			"3 equity, 4 revenue, 5 expense); got " + code)
	}
	// journal validates length, duplication and the chart cap itself; its
	// error is returned rather than swallowed so the caller sees which bound
	// they hit.
	if err := b.Open(code, name, kind); err != nil {
		panic("books: " + err.Error())
	}
}

// PostSimple posts the two-leg entry that is the overwhelmingly common case:
// debit one account, credit another, for the same amount.
//
// It exists beside PostMulti because the two-leg form cannot be mis-encoded.
// A caller who wants one debit and one credit should never have to get a leg
// specification's syntax right.
func PostSimple(cur realm, debitCode, creditCode string, amount int64, memo string) uint64 {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	b := mustOwnBook(cur)
	checkMemo(memo)
	if amount <= 0 {
		panic("books: amount must be positive")
	}
	if debitCode == creditCode {
		// journal would accept this and fold both legs, netting to zero
		// against one account. It is always a caller mistake, so it is
		// refused here rather than recorded as a no-op entry.
		panic("books: debit and credit accounts must differ")
	}
	postings := []journal.Posting{
		{Account: debitCode, Side: journal.Debit, Amount: amount},
		{Account: creditCode, Side: journal.Credit, Amount: amount},
	}
	id, err := b.Post(runtime.ChainHeight(), memo, postings)
	if err != nil {
		panic("books: " + err.Error())
	}
	return id
}

// PostMulti posts an entry of up to MaxLegsPerEntry legs, specified as a
// comma-separated list of colon-separated triples:
//
//	"1000:D:500,4000:C:300,4100:C:200"
//
// Side is D or C, case-insensitive. Amounts are positive integers in the
// owner's chosen unit. The string form is used because a crossing function
// cannot usefully take a slice over MsgCall; treasury_board encodes a list
// the same way.
//
// Debits must equal credits, and journal names which side is short.
//
// A leg may name an account another leg already named — journal folds them,
// and "1000:D:500,1000:C:200,4000:C:300" is a legitimate net +300 to 1000.
// What is refused is an entry in which EVERY leg names the same account: it
// balances only by netting to exactly zero, so it is the PostSimple mistake
// wearing the multi-leg syntax. The narrow rule is deliberate — a blanket
// "no repeated account" would reject the legitimate fold above.
func PostMulti(cur realm, legs, memo string) uint64 {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	b := mustOwnBook(cur)
	checkMemo(memo)
	postings := parseLegs(legs)
	if allOneAccount(postings) {
		panic("books: every leg names " + postings[0].Account +
			"; such an entry can only net to zero")
	}
	id, err := b.Post(runtime.ChainHeight(), memo, postings)
	if err != nil {
		panic("books: " + err.Error())
	}
	return id
}

// Reverse posts the mirror image of an existing entry in the caller's book,
// dated now. The original is left exactly as it was: this is a correction,
// not an edit.
//
// An entry may be reversed at most once. The reversal is itself an entry and
// may itself be reversed once.
func Reverse(cur realm, id uint64, memo string) uint64 {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	b := mustOwnBook(cur)
	checkMemo(memo)
	newID, err := b.Reverse(id, runtime.ChainHeight(), memo)
	if err != nil {
		panic("books: " + err.Error())
	}
	return newID
}

// CloseThrough seals the caller's book up to and including a height: no entry
// may afterwards be dated at or before it. The seal only ever moves forward.
//
// Passing a height at or beyond the current one seals the present and makes
// the book immediately unpostable until the chain advances past it. That is
// permitted — it is the owner's own book — and it is called out because it is
// the one way to brick a book without filling it.
func CloseThrough(cur realm, through int64) {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	b := mustOwnBook(cur)
	if err := b.Close(through); err != nil {
		panic("books: " + err.Error())
	}
}

// Rederive runs journal's STRONG invariant check over the caller's own book:
// it replays every posting of every entry and compares the result against
// both accumulators.
//
// It is a crossing function rather than a getter on purpose. The check is
// O(all postings ever) and the owner is the party who should pay for it —
// exposing it as a free read would let anyone make an owner's book expensive
// to query. IsBalanced is the cheap check and is a free read.
//
// Returns the replayed totals and whether they agree.
func Rederive(cur realm) (debits, credits int64, agrees bool) {
	refuseCoins(cur)
	b := mustOwnBook(cur)
	return b.RederiveFromJournal()
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Internals
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// mustOwnBook returns the CALLER'S book, and there is no variant that returns
// anyone else's.
//
// Authorization is structural rather than a check: the owner is derived from
// cur.Previous().Address() and no mutating function takes an owner parameter,
// so there is no address for a caller to forge. journal obligation 5.
//
// "No address to forge" is true of the SIGNATURE and was never true of the
// realm VALUE, which is the second half of the same Class 2 shape refuseCoins
// carries above — and the more consequential half, because this is the
// function that decides who owns the book rather than one that only refuses
// coins. The guard is a no-op today for the same reason as refuseCoins's:
// every call site forwards a crossing entrypoint's own first cur. It is kept
// for the same reason too, and its call-site count is likewise not written
// down. See AUDIT.md § O.
func mustOwnBook(cur realm) *journal.Book {
	if !cur.IsCurrent() {
		panic("books: mustOwnBook called with a realm that is not current")
	}
	owner := cur.Previous().Address()
	b, ok := books.Get(owner.String()).(*journal.Book)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: no book for this address; call OpenBook first")
	}
	return b
}

// book returns an owner's book for READ-ONLY use by the getters below.
//
// The pointer never leaves this file: every exported getter copies scalars or
// journal's by-value Account/Entry out of it. Obligation 4.
//
// avl.Tree.Get returns ONE value, not (any, bool): it cannot distinguish a
// nil-valued key from an absent one, which is the same trap journal's
// `reversed` field comment names from the other side. The two-value TYPE
// ASSERTION is what carries the existence answer here, in one traversal —
// `ok` is false for an absent key and for a present-but-wrong-typed value
// alike, and the second is impossible because only this file ever Sets.
// Do not "fix" this into Has-then-Get: that is two traversals for the same
// answer, and a reader would then owe themselves a proof that nothing can
// change between them.
func book(owner address) (*journal.Book, bool) {
	b, ok := books.Get(owner.String()).(*journal.Book)
	if !ok {
		return nil, false
	}
	return b, true
}

func mustBook(owner address) *journal.Book {
	b, ok := book(owner)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: no book for " + owner.String())
	}
	return b
}

// allOneAccount reports whether every posting names the same account.
//
// parseLegs has already refused a list shorter than two, so this is never
// asked of a single-element slice and the loop below always compares.
func allOneAccount(postings []journal.Posting) bool {
	for _, p := range postings[1:] {
		if p.Account != postings[0].Account {
			return false
		}
	}
	return true
}

func checkMemo(memo string) {
	if len(memo) > MaxMemoLen {
		panic("books: memo too long, max " + strconv.Itoa(MaxMemoLen))
	}
}

// parseLegs decodes the "code:side:amount,..." form of PostMulti.
//
// Every failure is a panic naming the offending leg by its 1-based position,
// because a caller who mistyped one triple in a list of eight needs to know
// which one. A silently-dropped leg would produce an unbalanced entry and the
// error would surface as "debits do not equal credits", which points at the
// wrong thing.
func parseLegs(legs string) []journal.Posting {
	if strings.TrimSpace(legs) == "" {
		panic("books: no legs given; format is \"1000:D:500,4000:C:500\"")
	}
	parts := strings.Split(legs, ",")
	if len(parts) < 2 {
		panic("books: an entry needs at least two legs")
	}
	if len(parts) > MaxLegsPerEntry {
		panic("books: too many legs, max " + strconv.Itoa(MaxLegsPerEntry))
	}
	out := make([]journal.Posting, 0, len(parts))
	for i, raw := range parts {
		at := " at leg " + strconv.Itoa(i+1)
		f := strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(raw), ":")
		if len(f) != 3 {
			panic("books: expected code:side:amount" + at)
		}
		code := strings.TrimSpace(f[0])
		if code == "" {
			panic("books: empty account code" + at)
		}
		var side journal.Side
		switch strings.ToUpper(strings.TrimSpace(f[1])) {
		case "D", "DR", "DEBIT":
			side = journal.Debit
		case "C", "CR", "CREDIT":
			side = journal.Credit
		default:
			panic("books: side must be D or C" + at)
		}
		amount, err := strconv.ParseInt(strings.TrimSpace(f[2]), 10, 64)
		if err != nil {
			panic("books: amount is not an integer" + at)
		}
		if amount <= 0 {
			panic("books: amount must be positive" + at)
		}
		out = append(out, journal.Posting{Account: code, Side: side, Amount: amount})
	}
	return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reads — all owner-scoped, by §4.2 of DISCOVERY.md
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// HasBook reports whether an address has opened a book.
func HasBook(owner address) bool { return books.Has(owner.String()) }

// Books is the number of books that exist. It is a count and nothing more:
// there is deliberately no way to enumerate them or aggregate across them.
func Books() int { return bookCount }

// OpenedAt is the height an owner's book was created.
func OpenedAt(owner address) int64 {
	h, ok := openedAt.Get(owner.String()).(int64)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: no book for " + owner.String())
	}
	return h
}

// Balance returns an account's balance in its NORMAL direction: positive
// means a debit balance for an asset or expense, and a credit balance for a
// liability, equity or revenue account.
//
// The second result distinguishes "no such account" from "zero".
func Balance(owner address, code string) (int64, bool) {
	b, ok := book(owner)
	if !ok {
		return 0, false
	}
	return b.Balance(code)
}

// RawBalance returns an account's debits minus credits, without the normal-
// direction sign convention. Exposed because a consumer reconciling against
// an external system usually wants the raw figure.
func RawBalance(owner address, code string) (int64, bool) {
	b, ok := book(owner)
	if !ok {
		return 0, false
	}
	a, found := b.Account(code)
	if !found {
		return 0, false
	}
	return a.RawDelta(), true
}

// AccountInfo returns an account's full state. journal returns Account BY
// VALUE, so this hands out a copy and no pointer into stored state.
func AccountInfo(owner address, code string) (name, kind string, debits, credits, balance int64) {
	a, ok := mustBook(owner).Account(code)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: no account " + code + " for " + owner.String())
	}
	return a.Name, a.Kind.String(), a.Debits, a.Credits, a.Balance()
}

// HasAccount reports whether an owner's chart contains a code.
func HasAccount(owner address, code string) bool {
	b, ok := book(owner)
	if !ok {
		return false
	}
	_, found := b.Account(code)
	return found
}

// Accounts is the number of accounts in an owner's chart.
func Accounts(owner address) int { return mustBook(owner).AccountCount() }

// Codes lists an owner's account codes in ascending code order, beginning at
// from INCLUSIVE and returning at most limit of them. A limit <= 0 returns
// nothing; a limit above the chart cap is clamped to it. Page by passing the
// last code received back as from, which re-reads that one code.
//
// # Why this exists, and why a counter was not enough
//
// Accounts above returns HOW MANY accounts a chart holds, and AccountInfo
// answers for a code the caller already knows. Neither lets a caller DISCOVER
// a code. Without this, a trial balance, a balance sheet and an income
// statement are all unbuildable from outside this realm however many times
// the chart is interrogated — a consumer could only report on accounts it had
// independently been told about, which for a chart the OWNER defines is no
// guarantee at all.
//
// # Why not simply export journal's iterator
//
// journal.Book has IterateAccounts(fn func(Account) bool), and exporting a
// realm entrypoint that takes fn from the caller would be the shortest code
// here and a security defect. A caller-supplied callback is the "callback
// params" class, and journal's own doc records the sharp edge: fn runs under
// the CALLING REALM'S STORAGE AUTHORITY, because the receiver is stamped with
// this realm's PkgID and a top-level fn has no declaring realm to anchor it
// elsewhere. A hostile fn would execute holding r/books' own authority.
//
// So the iterator stays internal and this returns VALUES. The closure below
// is written by this realm, appends to a local slice, and re-enters no
// mutator — which is exactly the condition journal's doc attaches to it.
//
// Codes is a free read and NOT a crossing function, so an owner does not pay
// for a consumer's paging. The cost ceiling is the chart cap: the underlying
// iterator has no seek, so `from` is honoured by skipping, and one call walks
// the whole chart however small limit is. A caller paging a 128-account chart
// 8 codes at a time therefore does 16 full walks, not one. That is a
// deliberate trade for not touching p/journal, whose Codes-with-seek would
// have re-opened Gate A and the sabotage sweeps for all three of its
// importers.
func Codes(owner address, from string, limit int) []string {
	b := mustBook(owner)
	if limit <= 0 {
		return nil
	}
	if limit > MaxAccountsPerBook {
		limit = MaxAccountsPerBook
	}
	out := make([]string, 0, limit)
	b.IterateAccounts(func(a journal.Account) bool {
		if a.Code < from {
			return false
		}
		out = append(out, a.Code)
		return len(out) >= limit
	})
	return out
}

// EntryCount is the number of entries in an owner's journal.
func EntryCount(owner address) int { return mustBook(owner).EntryCount() }

// EntryInfo returns one entry's header. Postings are returned separately by
// EntryLegs, because an entry's leg count is variable and a crossing-function
// return cannot carry a slice usefully.
func EntryInfo(owner address, id uint64) (at int64, memo string, legs int, reversalOf uint64, total int64) {
	e, ok := mustBook(owner).Entry(id)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: no entry " + strconv.FormatUint(id, 10))
	}
	return e.At, e.Memo, len(e.Postings), e.ReversalOf, e.Total()
}

// EntryLegs returns one entry's postings in the "code:side:amount,..." form
// PostMulti accepts, so an entry can be read and re-posted without a
// separate encoding.
func EntryLegs(owner address, id uint64) string {
	e, ok := mustBook(owner).Entry(id)
	if !ok {
		panic("books: no entry " + strconv.FormatUint(id, 10))
	}
	var sb strings.Builder
	for i, p := range e.Postings {
		if i > 0 {
			sb.WriteString(",")
		}
		sb.WriteString(p.Account)
		if p.Side == journal.Debit {
			sb.WriteString(":D:")
		} else {
			sb.WriteString(":C:")
		}
		sb.WriteString(strconv.FormatInt(p.Amount, 10))
	}
	return sb.String()
}

// IsReversed reports whether an entry has already been reversed. An entry may
// be reversed at most once, so this is also "may this still be reversed".
func IsReversed(owner address, id uint64) bool {
	return mustBook(owner).IsReversed(id)
}

// Totals returns an owner's running debit and credit totals.
func Totals(owner address) (debits, credits int64) {
	b := mustBook(owner)
	return b.TotalDebits(), b.TotalCredits()
}

// IsBalanced is the CHEAP invariant and this realm's live-verification probe.
//
// It compares two independently-maintained accumulators: the per-account
// totals summed across the chart, against the book's own running totals.
// They are written by different code paths, so a drift between them is a real
// defect and this finds it.
//
// What it does NOT do is re-read the journal, so a defect that corrupted both
// accumulators identically would survive it. That is Rederive's job, and
// Rederive is a crossing function because the owner should pay for it.
//
// And the ceiling on both: this proves the book is self-consistent, never
// that its entries describe real events. See the package doc.
func IsBalanced(owner address) bool { return mustBook(owner).IsBalanced() }

// Equation partitions an owner's chart BY ACCOUNT KIND: the summed raw delta
// of every debit-normal account (Asset, Expense), the same for every
// credit-normal one (Liability, Equity, Revenue), and whether the two offset.
//
// The two FIGURES are why this exists. They are the balance-sheet and
// income-statement partition of the chart, and no other accessor on this
// realm exposes it — Totals sums debits and credits without regard to kind,
// and AccountInfo answers one code at a time.
//
// # `offsets` IS A VACUOUS TRUE ON THIS REALM — do not quote it as a check
//
// Read this before using the third result for anything.
//
// The kind partition is TOTAL: journal's Kind is 1..5, IsDebitNormal is
// `k == Asset || k == Expense`, so every account lands in exactly one of the
// two sums and neither can omit one. Therefore
//
//	debitNormal + creditNormal  ==  sum of RawDelta over ALL accounts
//	                            ==  totalDebits - totalCredits
//
// and this realm refuses an entry that does not balance, so that difference
// is always zero. `offsets` is consequently TRUE for every book this realm
// can produce, and it is true for a chart whose accounts are classified
// nonsensically just as readily as for a correct one.
//
// This is the `HasValidSignature` shape §7b already names: a predicate that
// is a constant under current conditions, and so is evidence of nothing. The
// suite pins the vacuity deliberately rather than asserting true and reading
// green — a test that cannot fail is the thing that makes this dangerous.
//
// What `offsets` DOES still distinguish is arithmetic overflow, where journal
// returns (0, 0, false). So the honest reading is **"the two sums are
// trustworthy"**, never "the classification is right". A consumer wanting a
// genuine classification check must compare the kinds against its own
// expectation, which is the statement consumer's job and not this realm's.
//
// Both figures are RAW deltas (debits - credits), not normalised balances, so
// a debit-normal total is normally positive and a credit-normal total normally
// negative. A credit-normal account carrying a positive raw delta is a contra
// account, which is legitimate, and is why neither figure is forced
// non-negative.
func Equation(owner address) (debitNormal, creditNormal int64, offsets bool) {
	return mustBook(owner).AccountingEquation()
}

// ClosedThrough returns the height an owner's book is sealed to, and whether
// it has ever been closed. The second result matters: a book closed through
// height 0 is not the same as a book that has never been closed.
func ClosedThrough(owner address) (int64, bool) {
	return mustBook(owner).ClosedThrough()
}

// Capacity returns an owner's remaining headroom, so a consumer can see a
// full book coming rather than discovering it on a refused entry.
func Capacity(owner address) (accountsLeft, entriesLeft int) {
	b := mustBook(owner)
	return MaxAccountsPerBook - b.AccountCount(), MaxEntriesPerBook - b.EntryCount()
}

// Height is the chain height this realm dates entries with. Exposed so a
// caller can predict what CloseThrough will seal.
func Height() int64 { return runtime.ChainHeight() }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Render
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Render serves the index at "" and one owner's book at "<address>".
//
// Every caller-supplied string — account names and entry memos — goes through
// the ecosystem sanitizer. Free text reaching Render unescaped is the one
// injection-shaped surface a realm with no custody still has.
func Render(path string) string {
	p := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(path, "/"))
	if p == "" {
		return renderIndex()
	}
	return renderBook(address(p))
}

func renderIndex() string {
	var sb strings.Builder
	sb.WriteString("# books — a per-owner general ledger\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("Double-entry books. Append-only, corrected by reversal, ")
	sb.WriteString("and **no custody of any asset**.\n\n")

	sb.WriteString("> A book is a set of claims by its owner. With no custody ")
	sb.WriteString("there is no solvency invariant tying a balance to a real ")
	sb.WriteString("holding — a book can be internally perfect and describe ")
	sb.WriteString("nothing. The invariants here prove arithmetic, not truth.\n\n")

	sb.WriteString("## State\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("- books opened: **" + strconv.Itoa(bookCount) + "**\n")
	sb.WriteString("- chain height: **" + strconv.FormatInt(runtime.ChainHeight(), 10) + "**\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("There is deliberately no way to enumerate books or to ")
	sb.WriteString("aggregate across them: such a total would be the sum of ")
	sb.WriteString("every stranger's self-reported assertion.\n\n")

	sb.WriteString("## The chart-of-accounts convention\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("An account's **code determines its kind** — there is no kind ")
	sb.WriteString("parameter:\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("| code | kind | normal balance |\n")
	sb.WriteString("|---|---|---|\n")
	sb.WriteString("| `1xxx` | asset | debit |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| `2xxx` | liability | credit |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| `3xxx` | equity | credit |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| `4xxx` | revenue | credit |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| `5xxx` | expense | debit |\n\n")

	sb.WriteString("## Caps, per owner\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("| bound | value |\n|---|---|\n")
	sb.WriteString("| accounts per book | " + strconv.Itoa(MaxAccountsPerBook) + " |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| entries per book | " + strconv.Itoa(MaxEntriesPerBook) + " |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| legs per entry | " + strconv.Itoa(MaxLegsPerEntry) + " |\n\n")

	sb.WriteString("## Usage\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("```\nOpenBook()\nOpenAccount(\"1000\", \"Cash\")\n")
	sb.WriteString("OpenAccount(\"4000\", \"Sales\")\n")
	sb.WriteString("PostSimple(\"1000\", \"4000\", 5000, \"invoice 17 settled\")\n")
	sb.WriteString("PostMulti(\"1000:D:500,4000:C:300,4100:C:200\", \"split sale\")\n```\n\n")
	sb.WriteString("View a book at `" + RealmPath + ":<address>`.\n")
	return sb.String()
}

// cell prepares caller-supplied text for a GFM table cell, truncating it for
// display first. It is the `invoices` helper of the same name, adopted verbatim
// rather than re-derived, because the two realms face the identical slot.
//
// TableCell rather than InlineText, and the SLOT is the reason: these bytes
// land between two `|` delimiters, and an unescaped `|` opens a new column and
// shifts every column to its right. InlineText does not escape the pipe —
// deliberately, per the sanitizer's own doc, so that TableCell can escape it
// without double-escaping. Measured on onyx-1 with chain_id_bound: true:
//
//	InlineText("Cash | **forged** | x") -> `Cash | \*\*forged\*\* | x`   pipes LIVE
//	TableCell ("Cash | **forged** | x") -> `Cash \| \*\*forged\*\* \| x`  escaped
//
// This realm shipped InlineText here first and an adversarial audit found it.
// An owner could forge columns in their own book's chart or journal — and the
// victim is a THIRD PARTY reading that book, which attacks the one thing this
// realm exists to provide. The account CODE needs this as much as the name
// does: a GFM row is split before inline parsing runs, so a `|` inside the
// backtick span at the code column is not protected by the backticks, and
// kindFromCode constrains only code[0].
//
// Applied to caller-supplied text only. `Kind.String()` and the `note` column
// are realm-constructed from an enum and a uint64, so sanitizing them would be
// noise rather than slot-matching — unlike `invoices`, which passes a bech32
// address through this helper precisely because that value IS caller-derived.
//
// TRUNCATE BEFORE ESCAPING, and on a RUNE boundary. Escaping first and cutting
// afterwards can slice a `\|` in half and leave the cell ending in a bare
// backslash; cutting on a byte boundary can split a multi-byte rune and emit
// invalid UTF-8. By the time the sanitizer sees the string the damage is
// upstream of it, so neither is something it is asked to repair.
func cell(s string) string {
	const maxRunes = 48
	if r := []rune(s); len(r) > maxRunes {
		s = string(r[:maxRunes]) + "…"
	}
	return sanitize.TableCell(s)
}

func renderBook(owner address) string {
	b, ok := book(owner)
	if !ok {
		return "# books\n\nNo book for `" +
			sanitize.InlineText(owner.String()) + "`.\n"
	}

	var sb strings.Builder
	sb.WriteString("# Book of " + sanitize.InlineText(owner.String()) + "\n\n")

	// Invariant first, with its ceiling stated beside it rather than below.
	if b.IsBalanced() {
		sb.WriteString("**IsBalanced: true** — the per-account totals agree ")
		sb.WriteString("with the running totals. This is arithmetic ")
		sb.WriteString("self-consistency and says nothing about whether the ")
		sb.WriteString("entries describe real events.\n\n")
	} else {
		sb.WriteString("**IsBalanced: FALSE** — the accumulators have drifted. ")
		sb.WriteString("This is a defect; do not rely on any figure below.\n\n")
	}

	d, c := b.TotalDebits(), b.TotalCredits()
	sb.WriteString("| | |\n|---|---|\n")
	sb.WriteString("| total debits | " + strconv.FormatInt(d, 10) + " |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| total credits | " + strconv.FormatInt(c, 10) + " |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| accounts | " + strconv.Itoa(b.AccountCount()) + " / " +
		strconv.Itoa(MaxAccountsPerBook) + " |\n")
	sb.WriteString("| entries | " + strconv.Itoa(b.EntryCount()) + " / " +
		strconv.Itoa(MaxEntriesPerBook) + " |\n")
	if through, has := b.ClosedThrough(); has {
		sb.WriteString("| closed through | height " +
			strconv.FormatInt(through, 10) + " |\n")
	} else {
		sb.WriteString("| closed through | never closed |\n")
	}
	sb.WriteString("\n")

	// Chart of accounts, in ascending code order — avl, so deterministic.
	sb.WriteString("## Chart of accounts\n\n")
	if b.AccountCount() == 0 {
		sb.WriteString("_No accounts yet._\n\n")
	} else {
		sb.WriteString("| code | name | kind | debits | credits | balance |\n")
		sb.WriteString("|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n")
		b.IterateAccounts(func(a journal.Account) bool {
			sb.WriteString("| `" + cell(a.Code) + "` | " +
				cell(a.Name) + " | " + a.Kind.String() + " | " +
				strconv.FormatInt(a.Debits, 10) + " | " +
				strconv.FormatInt(a.Credits, 10) + " | " +
				strconv.FormatInt(a.Balance(), 10) + " |\n")
			return false
		})
		sb.WriteString("\nBalances are in each account's **normal direction**.\n\n")
	}

	// The most recent entries, newest first, bounded.
	sb.WriteString("## Journal\n\n")
	total := b.EntryCount()
	if total == 0 {
		sb.WriteString("_No entries yet._\n")
		return sb.String()
	}
	shown := total
	if shown > RenderEntryLimit {
		shown = RenderEntryLimit
	}
	// Entries are ids 1..nextID-1 and none are ever removed, so the newest
	// `shown` of them start at total-shown+1.
	from := uint64(total-shown) + 1
	recent := b.Entries(from, shown)

	sb.WriteString("| id | height | memo | legs | total | note |\n")
	sb.WriteString("|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n")
	for i := len(recent) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
		e := recent[i]
		note := ""
		if e.IsReversal() {
			note = "reverses #" + strconv.FormatUint(e.ReversalOf, 10)
		} else if b.IsReversed(e.ID) {
			note = "reversed"
		}
		memo := cell(e.Memo)
		if memo == "" {
			memo = "_none_"
		}
		sb.WriteString("| " + strconv.FormatUint(e.ID, 10) + " | " +
			strconv.FormatInt(e.At, 10) + " | " + memo + " | " +
			strconv.Itoa(len(e.Postings)) + " | " +
			strconv.FormatInt(e.Total(), 10) + " | " + note + " |\n")
	}
	if total > shown {
		sb.WriteString("\n_Showing the " + strconv.Itoa(shown) + " most recent of " +
			strconv.Itoa(total) + " entries._\n")
	}
	sb.WriteString("\nEntries are append-only. A wrong entry is corrected by ")
	sb.WriteString("posting its reversal, which leaves both readable.\n")
	return sb.String()
}

// Package fishlive is the person-versus-person sea.
// CommitMint pays 150 GNOT and stores only the caller and the commit height.
// RevealMint, once the chain is at least two blocks later, rolls rarity and trait
// from sha256 of the caller, the commit height, that fixed height plus two, and the id.
// Waiting longer does not change the roll. The player does not pass trait, size, or rarity.
// Swim stays off chain. Register locks at least 10 GNOT. Leave returns it after 5 seconds.
// Settle pays one covered stake. There is no machine fish and no FISH draw.
// A seated wallet can settle any other seated fish. The realm does not replay the bite.
// The five older commons stay on shoal/fish. This package does not move them.
package fishlive

import (
	"chain"
	"chain/banker"
	"chain/runtime"
	"chain/runtime/unsafe"
	"crypto/sha256"
	"strconv"
	"time"
)

const (
	denom          = "ugnot"
	mintFee  int64 = 150_000_000
	stakeMin int64 = 10_000_000
	quietMs  int64 = 5_000
	capFish        = 128
)

var (
	hpBase   = [6]int{100, 122, 148, 178, 214, 256}
	biteBase = [6]int{24, 30, 37, 45, 54, 66}
